menu "libtracer"

    # Per-transport module selection (#393) — mirrors the core CMake options
    # LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_{UDP,TCP,WS,CAN}. All default y, so a stock node compiles
    # the full transport set (byte-identical to before this knob existed). Turn one
    # OFF to shed that transport's translation units from libtracer.a for a node that
    # never creates a connection of that kind — the RAM-audit "compose the C6 for its
    # role" lever (shed unused transports), with NO in-source feature macros: which
    # transport compiles is decided by which TUs the build selects. A SPEC of a
    # trimmed `kind` then resolves to SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND (the factory is simply absent).

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_UDP
        bool "Build the UDP datagram transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `udp` transport (udp_transport_t + transport_udp.cpp
            + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node that never creates
            a `kind=udp` connection to drop those TUs from the archive.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_TCP
        bool "Build the TCP stream transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `tcp` transport (tcp_transport_t / transport_tcp_server
            + transport_tcp.cpp + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node
            that never creates a `kind=tcp` connection.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        bool "Build the WebSocket transport"
        default y
        select HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
        select HTTPD_WS_PRE_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT if HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
        help
            Compile the WebSocket plane. ON by default. Turn OFF on a node that
            neither serves nor dials a WebSocket link.

            HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT is SELECTED, not merely depended on (#963). It
            defaults n in ESP-IDF, and httpd_ws_link.cpp is gated on it in
            CMakeLists.txt — so before this select, a stock project that left
            this option at its default y got the WS client link, no WS SERVER
            link, and a bare `undefined reference to
            tr::net::httpd_ws_link_t::httpd_ws_link_t(...)` at link time naming
            no cause. The public header stays includable either way (it only
            names httpd_handle_t / httpd_req_t), so nothing fails earlier. This
            option's help promised the server link; now it delivers it.

            The `select` is what makes httpd_ws_link_t's admission predicate
            (set_admission_cb) reachable: esp_http_server answers the opening
            WebSocket GET itself and, once it has, does NOT call the URI handler
            for it (httpd_uri.c returns before `uri->handler` on the handshake
            branch), so the ONLY point at which a registered handler sees the
            parsed opening GET — method, URI and headers — is the server's
            ws_pre_handshake_cb, and that member exists only behind
            CONFIG_HTTPD_WS_PRE_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT. It is present at this
            component's ESP-IDF floor (>=5.5.5, see idf_component.yml), so there
            is no second tier behind it. Conditioned on CONFIG_HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
            because the upstream symbol depends on it and because that same
            config already gates whether httpd_ws_link.cpp compiles at all; a
            build without it selects nothing.

            WHICH plane you get is decided by the TARGET, not by this bool (#947):

              * chip targets get the IDF-NATIVE links only — esp_ws_client_link_t
                (on esp_transport_ws) and, with CONFIG_HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT=y,
                httpd_ws_link_t (adopts your running esp_http_server). ESP-IDF
                WebSocket never uses POSIX sockets: core's portable
                transport_ws_server / transport_ws_client are the HOST
                implementation and are NOT compiled here. They are not merely
                unreachable on lwIP, they are unusable — their scatter-gather
                egress asks sendmsg for MSG_NOSIGNAL, which lwip_sendmsg rejects
                with EOPNOTSUPP, and every data frame is then silently discarded
                while the handshake and PING/PONG still work.
              * the `linux` (POSIX host) target gets the portable pair, which is
                the right implementation there and the only one available (that
                target has no esp_http_server).

            Consequence on a chip target: no `ws` entry is registered in the
            built-in transport catalog, so a `:children[]` SPEC carrying `kind=ws`
            with no staged link answers SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND. Serve or dial WebSocket
            on silicon by constructing an IDF-native link and handing it to
            transport_vertex_t::provide_link.

    # WebSocket CLIENT timing (#1160). These three are the esp_ws_client_link_t recv
    # loop's cadence and its re-dial policy: POLICY, not derivations, and with no
    # per-link RAM behind them — so they are per-IMAGE Kconfig rather than constructor
    # arguments (which is what the RAM-bearing sizes got instead). Every default is the
    # historical literal, so a stock build compiles the values this link always had.
    #
    # The link's OTHER two bounds — the dial timeout and the write timeout — are
    # deliberately NOT here. They are derived from CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S, and
    # that derivation is what keeps one stalled peer inside a single watchdog window; a
    # knob over them would let a node be configured into a watchdog panic. Move the
    # watchdog instead. All five are readable at runtime via
    # esp_ws_client_link_t::timing().

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_READ_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "WS client: read timeout after a positive poll (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 60000
        default 100
        help
            How long esp_ws_client_link_t's recv thread waits for bytes that
            esp_transport_poll_read has already reported as readable. Short by
            design — the data is buffered in the stack, so this bounds a stall,
            it does not pace anything. Raise it only on a target whose lwIP
            hands the poll and the data to the recv thread far apart.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_POLL_MS
        int "WS client: recv-loop poll turn (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 10000
        default 200
        help
            One turn of the client recv loop: how long it parks in
            esp_transport_poll_read before re-checking the stop flag. This is the
            teardown-latency knob — a destructor cannot join the recv thread
            faster than one turn — traded against how often an idle link wakes
            the CPU. Lower it for a node that tears links down on a UI action;
            raise it on a battery node where an idle WS client is pure overhead.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_RECONNECT_BACKOFF_MS
        int "WS client: backoff before re-dialing (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 600000
        default 1500
        help
            Upper bound on the wait after a failed or lost connection before the
            recv thread dials again. An UPPER bound only: the wait is on a
            condition variable the destructor signals, so a teardown never pays
            it. Raise it for a fleet that must not hammer a peer that is down;
            lower it on a bench node chasing a peer reboot. It is also what a
            refused admission costs before the next dial, so a node whose peer
            gates admission pays this per rejected attempt.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_CAN
        bool "Build the CAN transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the CAN transport (transport_can.cpp portable framing/reassembly +
            the TWAI-backed can_link_t on chip targets, SocketCAN on the linux host).
            ON by default. Turn OFF on a node with no CAN bus — this also drops the
            TWAI driver glue (twai_link.cpp).

    config LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_LINKS
        bool "Build the link-liveness engine (self-healing dial connections)"
        default y
        help
            Compile the RFC-0014 S5 link-liveness engine (self_heal_link.cpp): a
            connection whose kind asks for it is minted DORMANT, dials on demand,
            and self-heals with backoff under its own worker thread.

            Turn OFF on a flash-constrained node that does not use one. Measured
            on an ESP32-C6 image: 4,336 B of reachable self_heal_link_t symbols,
            and 0 B of .dram0.bss (the engine is code, not a static table).

            NO BUILT-IN KIND ASKS FOR IT TODAY. The udp/tcp/ws factories construct
            eagerly, and an ESP node stages its links with provide_link, which
            bypasses the factory catalog entirely — so on a stock image the engine
            is unreachable and this is a pure saving. With it off, registering a
            self_heal_dial kind is REFUSED (the kind is not catalogued and a SPEC
            naming it answers SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND) rather than quietly downgraded.

    config LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_WORKER_STACK
        int "Link-liveness worker stack (bytes, 0 = pthread default)"
        range 0 32768
        default 0
        depends on LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_LINKS
        help
            Stack size for the link-liveness engine's worker thread, PER ENGINE-
            MANAGED CONNECTION. 0 means the platform default, which on IDF is
            CONFIG_PTHREAD_TASK_STACK_SIZE_DEFAULT taken from the heap for every
            such link — 12,288 B on the reporter's sdkconfig in #1470.

            The worker is the sole dialer and the sole liveness publisher, so its
            stack has to carry a connect() and the publish fan-out beneath it.
            Size it deliberately on a board with tens of KB of free heap. A value
            below the platform floor is ignored and the default stack is used.

    config LIBTRACER_VERTEX_LOCK_STRIPES
        int "Vertex lock-stripe count"
        range 1 256
        default 16
        help
            Number of process-wide mutex/condvar lock stripes shared by every
            graph vertex (libtracer #361 section 2). This is the ONLY global
            mutable buffer libtracer links into a node, and its cost has two
            halves that behave differently — independent, either way, of how
            many vertices you register:

              * STATIC RAM, reserved at link time and NOT lazy: N stripe structs
                plus N condvar handles of .bss. On a unicore build the component
                strips their false-sharing padding, so 16 stripes cost 128 B
                rather than 1,024 B (measured, esp32c6 -Os).
              * HEAP, and this half IS lazy: the FreeRTOS mutex behind a stripe's
                handle (~90 B) is created on that stripe's first lock, so a
                stripe no vertex has hashed onto costs no heap at all.

            The stripe a vertex rides is chosen by hashing its pinned address,
            so lowering the count only raises CONTROL-PLANE lock contention
            (ring trim, edge/ACL mutation, await wake); the lock-free LKV
            read/write hot path is unaffected. On a single-core chip target a
            small count (4-8) reclaims RAM at negligible contention cost; the
            host/multi-core default stays 16.

            The value is delivered as the ordinary constexpr
            tr::graph::kVertexLockStripes in a generated <libtracer/config.hpp>
            (ADR-0068) that shadows the checked-in default for every translation
            unit of the build — one shared header, so the stripe table's size can
            never diverge across TUs. (On ESP-IDF the table itself is a guarded
            function-local static, not the host's `inline constinit` one:
            libstdc++ makes std::mutex's constructor constexpr only where its
            gthreads port supports static init, which ESP-IDF's does not.)

endmenu
